Hi Pat,

It's a year since we put your kiosks in at the clinic, and our practice manag=
er asked me to
write up how it's gone, for you and for our own board. I thought you might fi=
nd it useful too.

What's worked. Patients picked it up faster than we expected. In the first mo=
nth our reception
staff helped about one patient in five; by the spring it was one in fifty, mo=
stly people who
had left their glasses at home. The large-text option made the biggest differ=
ence, and our
older patients now ask for it by name. Reception says the queue at 8:30 on a =
Monday is half the
length it was.

What's been harder. The badge printer jams when the label roll is nearly fini=
shed, about once a
fortnight. Your support team showed us how to clear it, and we now change the=
 roll a little
early, which has mostly solved it. The other thing is the wording on the seco=
nd screen, "State
the purpose of your visit". A few patients found it cold, and one complained =
that it sounded
like a border crossing. We changed it to "What brings you in today?" in the s=
ettings, which
helped.

Numbers. Over the year the two kiosks signed in a little over 21,000 visits. =
The busiest day was
the flu clinic in October, with 412. The kiosk was down twice: once for the r=
outer change in
March, which was our fault, and once for about an hour in June during your ma=
intenance window,
which you'd warned us about.

Our staff. Everyone on the front desk has now used the dashboard, and two of =
them use it daily
to see who's in the building. The fire marshals print the on-site list during=
 drills, which they
like. We'd still find a one-page guide to the dashboard useful for new starte=
rs; at the moment
they learn it by watching someone else.

What we'd like next year. We're opening two more clinics, as I mentioned in a=
n earlier email,
and we'd want the same setup there. Our board would also like a monthly summa=
ry of visits by
day and hour, so we can plan reception cover. If the dashboard can already do=
 that, a pointer
would be enough.

Support. When we've called, someone has answered within a few minutes, and th=
e two times we
needed an engineer the visit came within the week. The only slip was in Augus=
t, when a
replacement label roll was sent to our old address; it turned up three days l=
ate. We've since
checked that you have the clinic's right delivery address. The online help pa=
ges are
good, though the search sometimes brings up articles for the older kiosk mode=
l, which confused
one of our receptionists for an afternoon.

A small worry. Some patients use the kiosk without reading anything, by habit=
. If the screens
change, we'd want to warn reception a week before, so they can help people th=
rough it. That's
the reason for my one question.

Thanks again for a good first year.

One thing I do need to know soon: will the November software update change th=
e sign-in screen our patients see?

Best wishes,
Farah Malik
Practice administrator, Greyfriars Clinic
